course: geography
lesson: 10
title: Canada, Australia, New Zealand, Ireland and the rest of the English-speaking world
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: English spread from one small island to more than 50 countries mainly through trade, settlement and empire, the same colonial race you play out in EU5.
explain: In the game France and England fight over North America; in real life Canada kept both, which is why it has two official languages and French Quebec.
explain: The game shows sugar islands as rich prizes, but it hides the people forced to grow the sugar: most Jamaicans today descend from Africans enslaved on British plantations.
explain: Every "new" land already had people: First Nations, Inuit and Métis in Canada, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples in Australia for at least 50,000 years, and Māori in New Zealand, who arrived around 1250 to 1300, one of the last large lands humans settled.
explain: Ireland is a special case: next door to Britain, ruled by it for centuries, it became independent in 1922 and still feels the Great Famine, when a million people died.
fact: Canada, with its capital Ottawa, is the second largest country in the world at about 10 million square kilometres, yet has only about 41 million people, most living within a few hundred kilometres of the US border, where it is milder and the land can be farmed, while the north is very cold.
fact: Canada has two official languages, English and French, and most people in Quebec speak French because it began as New France, founded with Quebec City in 1608.
fact: Canada has the longest coastline of any country, more than 200,000 km, including its many Arctic islands.
fact: Canada's Indigenous peoples are the First Nations, the Inuit and the Métis, and for over a century many of their children were taken to residential schools, the last of which closed in 1996.
fact: Australia's capital is Canberra, built as a compromise between rival cities Sydney and Melbourne, and about 27 million people live in the country, mostly near the coast in the south-east.
fact: Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples have lived in Australia for at least 50,000 years, and British colonisation from 1788 brought disease, violence and the taking of their land.
fact: New Zealand, with its capital Wellington, at the southern tip of the North Island close to the South Island, and largest city Auckland, has two main islands, the North Island and the South Island, and about 5.3 million people.
fact: The Treaty of Waitangi, signed in 1840 between the British Crown and many Māori chiefs, is New Zealand's founding document; its English and Māori versions do not say the same thing about who holds power, which has caused arguments ever since; and Māori and New Zealand Sign Language are official languages by law, while English is used everywhere without a law naming it.
fact: The Republic of Ireland, with its capital Dublin and about 5.4 million people, became independent in 1922 and a republic in 1949, and Irish is its first official language though most people speak English every day, because centuries of English rule, English-only schooling and the famine pushed Irish back.
fact: During the Great Famine of 1845 to 1852, when the potato crop failed but other food was still shipped out of Ireland, about a million people in Ireland died and over a million emigrated, and the island still has fewer people today than in 1841.
fact: English is an official language in more than 50 countries, including Nigeria, Kenya, Ghana, Jamaica, Singapore, the Philippines and, alongside Hindi for the central government, India.
fact: English is spoken in Caribbean countries such as Jamaica, Barbados, and Trinidad and Tobago, most of whose people descend from Africans enslaved on British sugar plantations.
fact: In 1337, when EU5 starts, English rule in Ireland reached firmly only the area around Dublin called the Pale, while Gaelic lords ruled most of the island.
fact: In the Mabo decision of 1992, Australia's High Court ruled that the land had not belonged to no one when the British came, and that Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples could hold native title to it.
exercise: What is the capital of Canada? | Ottawa | Toronto | Montreal | Vancouver
exercise: Why do most Canadians live in the south of their country? | The north is very cold and the south is milder and farmable | The north belongs to the United States | The government forbids living in the north without a permit | The south is closer to Europe
exercise: Why is French spoken in Quebec? | It began as New France, founded with Quebec City in 1608 | France bought it in 1900 | French was chosen by a vote of all its people in 1990 | Quebec is still a part of France, like Paris or Lyon
exercise: Which country has the longest coastline? | Canada | Australia | Russia, the largest country | Indonesia
exercise: Name Canada's three groups of Indigenous peoples. | First Nations, Inuit and Métis | Māori, Inuit and Métis | First Nations, Sami and Inuit | Aboriginal, Inuit and Cree
exercise: When did the last residential school in Canada close? | 1996 | 1867 | 1920 | 1950
exercise: What is the capital of Australia? | Canberra | Sydney | Melbourne | Perth
exercise: Why was Canberra built instead of choosing Sydney or Melbourne? | As a compromise between the two rival cities | Because Sydney was too hot in summer for the parliament | Because the Queen chose its name | Because it was on the coast
exercise: For how long have Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples lived in Australia? | At least 50,000 years | About 1,000 years, since the first farms | Only since the British ships came in 1788 | About 500 years, since the end of the Middle Ages
exercise: What is the capital of New Zealand? | Wellington | Auckland | Christchurch | Sydney
exercise: What is the Treaty of Waitangi? | New Zealand's founding document, signed with Māori chiefs in 1840 | A treaty ending a war with Australia | A trade deal with France in 1900 | A law making English official
exercise: Which languages are official in New Zealand by law? | Māori and New Zealand Sign Language | English only | English and French, the same as in Canada | Māori only
exercise: What is the capital of the Republic of Ireland? | Dublin | Belfast | Cork | Galway
exercise: When did Ireland become a republic? | 1949 | 1922 | 1801 | 1998
exercise: How many people died in Ireland's Great Famine? | About a million | About ten thousand | About ten million | About a hundred thousand
exercise: In about how many countries is English an official language? | More than 50 | Exactly 5 | About 12 | More than 190
exercise: Why do most people in Jamaica have African ancestors? | Africans were enslaved and shipped to its sugar plantations | They chose to move there for jobs in 1900 | Africa owned Jamaica | Jamaica was joined to Africa by land until about 1600
exercise: How much of Ireland did England rule firmly in 1337? | Only the area around Dublin, the Pale | All of the island, from coast to coast | Nothing at all | Only the far west, around Galway
test: If a game map painted all of Ireland in English colours in 1337, what was the real picture? | Gaelic lords ruled most of the island outside the Pale | England ruled every county | Scotland ruled the island | Ireland was nearly empty after the Black Death killed everyone
test: Why do most Irish people speak English rather than Irish at home? | Centuries of English rule, schooling and the famine pushed Irish back | Irish was never spoken in Ireland | English is the first official language | Irish was banned by the EU when Ireland joined in 1973
test: Why did food leaving Ireland during the famine make people so angry? | People were starving while food was shipped away | The food was poisoned | Ships took only the best potatoes | The food went to France as a gift from the British king
test: does Ireland have more people now than before the famine? | No, the island still has fewer than in 1841 | Yes, ten times more | Yes, exactly double the number it had back in 1841 | No, nobody lives there now
test: Why do islands add so much to a country's coastline? | Every island has its own shore all the way round | Islands make the sea deeper | Islands are counted twice | The sea pushes the coast further out around each of them
test: Why does Canada now teach children about residential schools? | So the harm done to Indigenous children is known and not repeated | Because they were popular schools | Because Canada still runs them in the far north for Inuit children | Because they taught French
test: What did the Mabo decision of 1992 say about the idea that Australia had belonged to no one? | It was wrong: Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples had rights to their land | It was right: the land was empty | It gave Australia back to Britain | It made Canberra the capital
test: Why might a capital in the middle of the two main islands make sense for New Zealand? | It is easy to reach from both islands | It is the biggest city | It is the warmest city in the country | It is the closest city to Australia
test: Why did the two language versions of the Treaty of Waitangi cause problems? | They did not say the same thing about who held power | Nobody could read either version | The Māori version was lost at sea | Both versions were written in French by the missionaries
test: Why is English useful in a country such as Nigeria, where hundreds of languages are spoken? | It lets people from different language groups understand each other | It is the only language anyone there speaks | Nigerian languages have no words for numbers | It was chosen in 2025
test: In EU5 the sugar islands are just rich trade goods. What does the game leave out? | The enslaved people forced to grow the sugar | The price of sugar | The ships that carried it | The islands' weather and the hurricanes that hit them each year
exam: Identify the second most extensive sovereign state in the world. | Canada | The United States of America | The People's Republic of China | Australia
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the circumstances of the foundation of Canberra? | It was constructed as a compromise between Sydney and Melbourne | It was the first British settlement in Australia | It is the most populous Australian city | It was founded by Dutch navigators upon the southern coast
exam: Ascertain the instrument regarded as the founding document of New Zealand. | The Treaty of Waitangi of 1840 | The Treaty of Paris of 1783 | The Act of Union of 1707 | The Statute of Westminster of 1931
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Great Famine in Ireland. | Approximately one million persons perished and over one million emigrated | No deaths whatsoever were recorded | It occurred in the fourteenth century | It increased the population of the island
